
  This matters more than it sounds. It gets baked into the core [38;5;153mlog()[39m call, and changing it later means re-auditing
  every call site in the codebase — so it's worth getting right before anything is written. The concrete risk here is
  not hypothetical: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m today logs the username on every login attempt and has a password field one line away, and
  the export flow means these logs will eventually be mailed around as a file.

  - [1mDeny-list[0m — redact keys matching known-sensitive patterns ([38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauth[39m, [38;5;153mcookie[39m, …); everything
    else is logged as-is. Easy to adopt, reads naturally, nothing surprising goes missing from your logs. The failure
    mode is silent and permanent: a field nobody thought of ([38;5;153mpwd[39m, [38;5;153mssn[39m, [38;5;153mapiKey2[39m, a whole request body nested three levels
    down) leaks, and you find out when the log file is already in someone's inbox.
  - [1mAllow-list[0m — only explicitly permitted fields are recorded; everything else becomes [38;5;153m[redacted][39m unless declared. Safe
    by default, and the failure mode is loud and harmless — a missing field in a log, which you notice and fix. Costs
    friction: every new field someone wants to log needs a one-line declaration, and people will grumble the first few
    times.
  - [1mNo structured redaction[0m — callers are responsible for not passing sensitive data. Zero machinery. I'd argue against
    it: it's what the code does today, and it's why the username is already being logged.

  My recommendation: [1mallow-list[0m. For a subsystem whose whole purpose is to produce files that get handed to other
  people, I want the mistake mode to be "a log is less useful than it could be" rather than "a credential left the
  building." The friction is real but it's front-loaded and small — this codebase has a handful of log sites, so the
  declarations cost minutes now rather than an audit later.
